_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_ | _⚡ Quick win_

**Multi-value response headers collapse to the last entry.**

`set_prop(&headers_obj, k, …)` writes via `Reflect.set`, which overwrites any prior property with the same key. Servers commonly emit multiple `Set-Cookie`, `Vary`, `Link`, or `WWW-Authenticate` headers, and `resp.headers: Vec<(String, String)>` already preserves duplicates — but this serialiser silently flattens them, dropping all but the last value. For `Set-Cookie` in particular this is observably wrong and will break any caller relying on multi-cookie responses.

<details>
<summary>♻️ Suggested fix: turn duplicate keys into arrays</summary>

```diff
-    let headers_obj = Object::new();
-    for (k, v) in &resp.headers {
-        set_prop(&headers_obj, k, &JsValue::from_str(v))?;
-    }
+    let headers_obj = Object::new();
+    for (k, v) in &resp.headers {
+        let key = JsValue::from_str(k);
+        let new_val = JsValue::from_str(v);
+        let existing = Reflect::get(&headers_obj, &key).ok();
+        let to_set = match existing {
+            Some(prev) if prev.is_undefined() || prev.is_null() => new_val,
+            Some(prev) if prev.is_array() => {
+                let arr: js_sys::Array = prev.unchecked_into();
+                arr.push(&new_val);
+                arr.into()
+            }
+            Some(prev) => {
+                let arr = js_sys::Array::new();
+                arr.push(&prev);
+                arr.push(&new_val);
+                arr.into()
+            }
+            None => new_val,
+        };
+        set_prop(&headers_obj, k, &to_set)?;
+    }
```

Alternatively, use a `Map` (preserves insertion order and is what the browser's `Headers` uses internally) and let the JS side reconstruct a real `Headers` from it.
