`@jstuczyn` Agreed — here's a concrete sketch of the state machine approach:

Add a `closed` flag to `WasmTcpStream` to distinguish "gracefully finished" from "abruptly torn down":

```rust
pub struct WasmTcpStream {
    pub(crate) stack: Arc<Mutex<SmoltcpStack>>,
    pub(crate) handle: SocketHandle,
    pub(crate) notify: ReactorNotify,
    closed: bool, // set only when poll_close reaches Closed/TimeWait
}
```

Then `poll_close` drives the FIN handshake to completion before returning `Ready`:

```rust
fn poll_close(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<io::Result<()>> {
    let mut s = self.stack.lock().unwrap();
    let socket = s.sockets.get_mut::<smoltcp_tcp::Socket>(self.handle);

    // Initiate graceful shutdown — idempotent once already in a FIN state.
    socket.close();

    match socket.state() {
        smoltcp_tcp::State::Closed | smoltcp_tcp::State::TimeWait => {
            // FIN handshake complete; mark as gracefully closed so Drop skips abort().
            drop(s);
            self.closed = true;
            let _ = self.notify.unbounded_send(());
            Poll::Ready(Ok(()))
        }
        _ => {
            // The send side drives the FIN handshake (ACK of our FIN arrives on
            // the send path), so register a send waker and nudge the reactor.
            socket.register_send_waker(cx.waker());
            drop(s);
            let _ = self.notify.unbounded_send(());
            Poll::Pending
        }
    }
}
```

And `Drop` skips `abort()` when the socket already completed a graceful close:

```rust
impl Drop for WasmTcpStream {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        let mut s = self.stack.lock().unwrap();
        if !self.closed {
            // No graceful close completed — send RST to avoid the remote
            // hanging waiting for data/FIN that will never arrive.
            s.sockets
                .get_mut::<smoltcp_tcp::Socket>(self.handle)
                .abort();
        }
        s.sockets.remove(self.handle);
    }
}
```

One nuance: after `socket.close()` is called smoltcp needs at least one `Interface::poll()` cycle to actually send the FIN and advance the state, so the `notify.unbounded_send(())` on the `Poll::Pending` path is important to ensure the reactor ticks promptly rather than waiting for the next deadline.

Would you like me to open a follow-up issue to track this, or plan the code change directly in this PR?

